## Data Modeling

### 1. Entity Relationship Model

**Core Concepts:**

**Standard Objects (Out-of-box):**
```
Document
├─ Properties: id, name, classification, created_date, owner
├─ Relationships: Has versions, has workflows, has permissions
└─ Lifecycle: Draft → Review → Approved → Published

Binder (Collections of documents)
├─ Properties: id, name, description, type
├─ Relationships: Contains documents, has versions
└─ Purpose: Organize related documents

User
├─ Properties: id, email, name, department
├─ Relationships: Creates documents, approves, reviews
└─ State: Active, Inactive, Disabled

Group
├─ Properties: id, name, description, members
├─ Relationships: Contains users, has permissions
└─ Purpose: Simplify permission management
```

**Custom Objects (Example - Clinical Trial):**
```
Study Object:
├─ Properties:
│  ├─ study_id__v: Unique study identifier
│  ├─ protocol__v: Study protocol
│  ├─ phase__v: Clinical phase (I, II, III, IV)
│  ├─ indication__v: Disease being studied
│  ├─ status__v: Active, Completed, Terminated
│  ├─ sponsor__v: Sponsoring company
│  └─ enrollment_target__v: Target participant count
│
├─ Relationships:
│  ├─ 1:N with Site (Study has many Sites)
│  ├─ 1:N with Investigator
│  └─ 1:1 with Protocol Document
│
└─ Lifecycle:
   ├─ Draft (Planning)
   ├─ Active (Enrolling)
   ├─ Completed (Data locked)
   └─ Closed (Archived)

Site Object (nested under Study):
├─ Properties:
│  ├─ site_id__v: Site identifier
│  ├─ location__v: Physical location
│  ├─ investigator__v: Principal investigator
│  ├─ enrollment_actual__v: Actual enrollment count
│  └─ site_status__v: Recruiting, Closed
│
└─ Relationships:
   ├─ Many:1 with Study
   └─ 1:N with Visit (Site has many Visits)

Visit Object (nested under Site):
├─ Properties:
│  ├─ visit_date__v: Scheduled visit date
│  ├─ actual_date__v: Actual visit date
│  ├─ participants_attended__v: Number attended
│  └─ status__v: Scheduled, Completed, Cancelled
│
└─ Relationships:
   └─ Many:1 with Site
```

### 2. Normalization Principles

**Avoid Data Redundancy:**

```
❌ Bad: Store redundant data
Study Object:
├─ study_id: ST001
├─ sponsor_name: "Pharma Corp"
├─ sponsor_address: "123 Main St"
├─ sponsor_phone: "555-1234"
├─ investigator_name: "Dr. Smith"
├─ investigator_email: "dr.smith@hospital.com"
├─ site_name: "General Hospital"
└─ site_address: "456 Oak Ave"

Problem: If sponsor address changes, must update all studies


✓ Good: Use relationships and lookup fields
Study Object:
├─ study_id: ST001
├─ sponsor__v: [Lookup to Sponsor object: SP001]
└─ sites__v: [Multi-select lookup: Sites under this study]

Sponsor Object:
├─ sponsor_id: SP001
├─ name: "Pharma Corp"
├─ address: "123 Main St"
└─ phone: "555-1234"

Benefit: Single source of truth, easier maintenance
```

### 3. Metadata Strategy

**Designing Custom Metadata:**

```
Principle 1: Every metadata field must serve a business purpose
├─ Classification → Determine access and workflow rules
├─ Therapeutic area → Filter documents by medical specialty
├─ Status → Track document progress
└─ Review date → Identify content requiring refresh

Principle 2: Use field types appropriately
├─ Text: Flexible, but hard to search
├─ Dropdown: Standardized values, easy to report on
├─ Multi-select: Multiple values (tags)
├─ Lookup: Reference to another object
├─ Formula: Auto-calculated values
├─ Date: Temporal data (expiration, review dates)
└─ User: Track who owns/approves

Principle 3: Plan for reporting
├─ Indexed fields: Used in searches and filters
├─ Aggregatable fields: Used in roll-ups
├─ Sortable fields: Used in sorting results
└─ Reportable fields: Included in reports

Example: Regulatory Document
Required Metadata:
├─ therapeutic_area__v: Dropdown (indexed, reportable)
├─ product_name__v: Lookup (indexed, sortable)
├─ indication__v: Multi-select (indexed, reportable)
├─ regulatory_status__v: Dropdown (indexed, sortable, reportable)
├─ submission_date__v: Date (indexed, sortable, reportable)
